--- !u!114 &-4253798941949906434
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'An important tool to master is the <b>Light Explorer,</b> under <b>Window</b> > <b>Rendering.</b>


      It provides a filtered view for all lighting-related GameObjects, from <b>Lights</b> to <b>Reflection Probes,</b> as well as <b>Volumes</b> and <b>Emissive Materials.</b> It is therefore extremely useful in large scenes that may contain a very large number of objects, without having to solely rely on the <i>Hierarchy</i> window.


      The <b>Light Explorer</b> also lets you tune key properties directly within the tool, such as light intensities and colors.'
